  <!-- About Me -->
  <section class="about-page bg-dark py-1 text-white">
    <div class="container mb-5 mt-0 my-md-5">
      <div class="row align-items-center">
        <div class="col-12 col-xl-6 d-flex justify-content-center my-4">
          <img class="responsive-img rounded-3" alt="My Picture">
        </div>
        <div class="col-12 col-xl-6">
          <div class="row mt-3">
            <div class="col-lg-12">
              <h2 class="text-primary text-center">Between Teaching and Technology</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="col-lg-12 about-paragraph">
              <p>I’m Ben Weaver. I’ve been teaching for a dozen years and currently teach 5th grade science.
                The tech side started when I got handed a computer science class and had to stay a week ahead
                of the students, which turned out to be an excellent way to learn something.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="col-lg-12 about-paragraph">
              <p>Since then it’s mostly been a matter of following whatever I got curious about. I earned my
                CompTIA A+ and I’m working through Network+ now, with Security+ after it. Plenty of things
                haven’t stuck. I spent a while on malware analysis and reverse engineering before admitting
                the math wasn’t there yet, so I backed up and started working through discrete math instead.
                I’d rather say that plainly than pretend the path was straight.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="col-lg-12 about-paragraph">
              <p>What has stuck: a homelab built almost entirely from secondhand and e-waste hardware, running
                the services I’d otherwise be renting from someone else. Segmented networks, my own DNS, my
                own backups, and a steady supply of self-inflicted problems to fix. Alongside that there’s a
                lot of soldering, ESP32 boards, and things coming off a 3D printer that mostly cooperates. I
                care about privacy and civic transparency, and occasionally do something about it locally.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="col-lg-12 about-paragraph">
              <p>The common thread is that I learn things by building them, and I write down what happened,
                including the parts that didn’t work, which are usually the more useful half. That’s what this
                site is for. Most of it ends up in the
                <a class="text-secondary" href="/projects.html">projects</a> or the
                <a class="text-secondary" href="/blog.html">blog</a>.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

